<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aranuka</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aranuka is a small atoll in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiribati">Kiribati</a>, near Abemama, made up of two main islets around a lagoon. In the 19th century the rulers of Abemama, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tem-baiteke">Tem Baiteke</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tem-binoka">Tem Binoka</a>, brought it under their kingdom, which was absorbed into the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of the Gilbert Islands in 1892.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aranuka.jpg" alt="Aranuka" width="320" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of USA, Government of Kiribati · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:10_Map_of_Aranuka,_Kiribati.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Aranuka is an atoll of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiribati">Kiribati</a>, just north of the equator in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a>. 6 square kilometres and had a population of 1,057 in 2010. It is triangular and mostly formed by two large islands, Buariki and Takaeang, both unusually large for an atoll of this size. 36 kilometres west of the main island.</p><p>Long sandbanks join the islands on the northern side and an underwater reef crest on the southern side, which also has a wide pass into the central lagoon. Boats can use the pass in moderate weather, though tidal streams are strong at several points. The lagoon is not rich in shellfish.</p><p>One road runs along the lagoon side of the island, where all the villages stand. Aranuka had only three villages from its first settlement until 1978, when a small fourth, Baurua, was founded. It is known for an old stand of Rhizophora stylosa mangroves that have grown as tall as coconut trees and are a breeding site for birds.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By local tradition Aranuka is the central island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiribati">Kiribati</a> and the place where the God of the ancestors, Nareau, began the formation and separation of all the islands. For this reason it was formerly called Ananuka, meaning the middle of it. The first Europeans recorded as visiting were Thomas Gilbert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-marshall">John Marshall</a>, sailing the First Fleet vessels Charlotte and Scarborough, in June 1788. The US Exploring Expedition surveyed the island in 1841.</p><p>At some point between 1860 and the late 1880s, Aranuka and Kuria were conquered by the paramount chief of Abemama. A former ruler of the two islands, Ten Temea, gave them up and left for Maiana, another island of the group. They were afterwards ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tem-binoka">Tem Binoka</a>. A post office opened on Aranuka around 1924.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Aranuka Airport lies near the northern tip of Buariki and has two flights a week from South Tarawa, which also link to Kuria. Charter boats also serve the island. Visitors come for its beaches and for swimming and snorkelling at the uninhabited islets, and the island council runs a guesthouse.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aranuka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tem Baiteke, using firearms obtained from traders, brought the nearby atolls of <b>Aranuka</b> and Kuria under his rule, creating a small island kingdom unlike the council-based societies of the other Gilberts.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-abemama">Kingdom of Abemama</a></li></ul></section>
